module Core

import BuilderQuery
### Write access to two different entities at once, so one can be changed while an element of the other is held.
### Made by {multimodify} and opened with `using ... as (a, b)`, which binds one {Modifying[T]} token per entity.
### :typeparam T: The entity type being modified.
### :see: {Modifying[T]}, {MultiAmending[T, TPolicy]}
record MultiModifying[T]
obeys Enterable
needs EntityType T
    first: Modifying[T]
    second: Modifying[T]

### Opens write access to two different entities at once: `using multimodify(first: a, second: b) as (x, y)`. Two
### tokens a routine takes may be the same entity, so the builder doesn't let one change shape while an element of
### the other is held. The two tokens `multimodify` hands out were checked to be different, so it does.
### :param first: The entity the first token modifies.
### :param second: The entity the second token modifies.
### :returns: A scoped `MultiModifying[T]` that binds the two tokens.
### :throws: {AliasedTokensError} when the two are the same entity.
routine multimodify![T](first: Modifying[T], second: Modifying[T]) -> MultiModifying[T]
needs EntityType T
    if first === second
        throw AliasedTokensError()
    danger
        return MultiModifying[T](first: first, second: second)

### Called on entry to the `using` block: hands out the two tokens.
### :returns: The two tokens, in the order the entities were given.
routine MultiModifying[T].enter() -> (Modifying[T], Modifying[T])
    return (me.first, me.second)

### Called on every exit from the `using` block: there is nothing to release.
routine MultiModifying[T].exit()
    return
